  <div class="section">

    <h1>From junior champion to world bridge champion.</h1>

    <div class="intro-with-photo">
      <div class="intro-text">
        <p class="lead">Bridge is in my blood. It has shaped who I am and where I stand today. Bridge has taught me a great deal, taken me all over the world — and introduced me to the love of my life. And now it's my work.</p>
      </div>
      <img class="portrait-photo" src="/images/richard-portret.webp" alt="Richard Ritmeijer" width="200" height="250" />
    </div>

    <hr />

    <h2>How it started</h2>
    <p>I was eleven when my grandmother won a prize at a bridge tournament my mother organized. My father — a bridge teacher himself — taught me the rules. A year later, I won a prize too. I was hooked.</p>
    <p>Not long after, I played in the Dutch Junior Championship. I won it convincingly. Bridge was no longer just a hobby. I represented the Netherlands multiple times at European and World Championships, both as a junior and as an adult.</p>

    <h2>University and the subtop</h2>
    <p>I was accepted to University College Utrecht — partly because I promised to start a bridge club there. I did. I taught fellow students and played in the subtop myself. Later, in Maastricht, I spent my final years playing on the Dutch junior national team. My partner and I performed so well that we were invited to the national B-squad — but our partnership ended.</p>

    <h2>Magie — and a new chapter</h2>
    <p>In 2008 I met my now wife: the Czech player Magie Tichá. When she moved to Groningen to study in 2010, we started playing for fun at De Zeerob. We performed well together right away. In 2011 we came fourth in the Master Class Pairs — and third at the European Mixed Pairs Championship in Poznań. It was clear: we were a partnership to watch.</p>

